CloseThreadpoolIo must not free a threadpool IO object that the completion
thread is already releasing.

TpReleaseIoCompletion() set shutting_down and, when pending_count and
skipped_count were both 0, called tp_ioqueue_unlock(). That function asserts
ioqueue.objcount (threadpool.c:2102) and then drops the object. The completion
thread does the same drop when it dequeues a packet, sees shutting_down, and
finds pending_count already 0. CancelThreadpoolIo() produces that state: it
decrements pending_count while the completion is still queued. Whichever side
runs second aborts in the assert, or frees the object twice.

Scam With Your Friends (Unity 6000.3, Mono) hits this on every Kolkata OS
recording teardown. The recorder disposes its audio named pipe from
KolkataRecordingAudioSession.WriteAudio (KolkataWebMRecorder.cs:1729), Mono
calls CloseThreadpoolIo, and ntdll aborts. Three crashes on 2026-10-18
(13:04, 13:12, 13:26 UTC) on engine x64-crossover26.3-r4, macOS 27, Apple
Silicon: the faulting instruction is RtlRaiseStatus from _assert, the caller
is tp_object_prepare_shutdown, and the return into TpReleaseIoCompletion is
the instruction after that call. The r4 ntdll strings are
"../src/sources/wine/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c", "ioqueue.objcount", line
0x836. A later crash the same day was in d3d11 and is not this bug.

CloseThreadpoolIo now only marks the object and posts one completion,
STATUS_THREADPOOL_RELEASED_DURING_OPERATION, with the object as key. The
completion thread ignores packets for an object that is already marked and
releases it exactly once, when that marker is dequeued. Packets queued ahead
of the marker are skipped rather than dispatched into a freed object.
tp_ioqueue_unlock() no longer asserts when the count is already 0, so a
cleanup-group shutdown cannot abort the process on the same line.

Not yet built or run in the game. Upstream used this single-owner release in
Paul Gofman's April 2021 threadpool IO queue (wine-devel, "Release IO thread
pool object from ioqueue_thread_proc"); the July 2021 can_destroy path that
Wine 11 and current master still ship is what races. The lost GetLastError()
that makes the game log "recording audio pipe failed: Success" on r4 is
patch 0011, which a build of this series already includes.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c	2026-10-18 20:23:16
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/threadpool.c	2026-10-18 20:23:16
@@ -1552,7 +1552,20 @@
 
         TRACE( "io %p, iosb.Status %#lx.\n", io, iosb.Status );
 
-        if (io && (io->shutdown || io->u.io.shutting_down))
+        /* TpReleaseIoCompletion() posts one marker and does not free the
+         * object. Packets already queued in front of it must not release
+         * it, and must not run a callback on it either. */
+        if (io && io->shutdown)
+        {
+            if (iosb.Status == STATUS_THREADPOOL_RELEASED_DURING_OPERATION && ioqueue.objcount)
+            {
+                --ioqueue.objcount;
+                TRACE( "Releasing io %p.\n", io );
+                tp_object_release( io );
+            }
+            io = NULL;
+        }
+        else if (io && io->u.io.shutting_down)
         {
             RtlEnterCriticalSection( &io->pool->cs );
             if (!io->u.io.pending_count)
@@ -2099,7 +2112,11 @@
 
     RtlEnterCriticalSection( &ioqueue.cs );
 
-    assert(ioqueue.objcount);
+    if (!ioqueue.objcount)
+    {
+        RtlLeaveCriticalSection( &ioqueue.cs );
+        return;
+    }
 
     if (!io->shutdown && !--ioqueue.objcount)
         NtSetIoCompletion( ioqueue.port, 0, 0, STATUS_SUCCESS, 0 );
@@ -2874,21 +2891,19 @@
 void WINAPI TpReleaseIoCompletion( TP_IO *io )
 {
     struct threadpool_object *this = impl_from_TP_IO( io );
-    BOOL can_destroy;
 
     TRACE( "%p\n", io );
 
-    RtlEnterCriticalSection( &this->pool->cs );
-    this->u.io.shutting_down = TRUE;
-    can_destroy = !this->u.io.pending_count && !this->u.io.skipped_count;
-    RtlLeaveCriticalSection( &this->pool->cs );
-
-    if (can_destroy)
-    {
-        tp_object_prepare_shutdown( this );
-        this->shutdown = TRUE;
-        tp_object_release( this );
+    /* The completion thread releases the object when it dequeues the marker
+     * posted here. Freeing on this thread races that release. */
+    RtlEnterCriticalSection( &ioqueue.cs );
+    if (ioqueue.objcount && !this->shutdown)
+    {
+        this->shutdown = TRUE;
+        NtSetIoCompletion( ioqueue.port, (ULONG_PTR)this, 0,
+                STATUS_THREADPOOL_RELEASED_DURING_OPERATION, 1 );
     }
+    RtlLeaveCriticalSection( &ioqueue.cs );
 }
 
